            "About correlation",
            "How calculated",
            "Save this visualization",
            "Elevated blood lead levels (age 16+)",
            "Blood lead levels over 10 mg/dL among people age 16+. In New York City, the most common cause of lead poisoning in non-pregnant adults is from working in the construction. Workers are exposed through lead dust or fumes during work that disturbs old lead paint. Workers in other industries, as well as hobbyists, may be at risk if they work with lead-containing metal, paint, pigments, ceramic glazes or bullets. Adults can also be exposed to lead through lead-contaminated products, like some imported health remedies, spices, foods, pottery and cosmetics.",
            "Hospitalizations billed to workers' compensation",
            "Hospitalizations billed to workers' compensation. Workers' compensation provides medical care for workers injured or become ill as a direct result of their job. Hospitalizations billed to workers' comp represent only a portion of work-related injuries that require hospital care. Workers' compensation policies vary from state-to-state.",
            "Asbestosis hospitalizations",
            "Hospitalizations for asbestosis. Asbestos is a naturally-occurring mineral which was commonly used as an insulation material until the 1970s. Inhaling asbestos fibers can result in the chronic lung disease called asbestosis, a type of pneumoconiosis. New York City construction, insulation, and shipyard workers who installed, disturbed, or removed asbestos without respiratory protection are at risk. Typically it takes more than ten years before symptoms develop. The use of proper protective masks is key to preventing the disease, since there is no treatment.",
            "Pneumoconiosis hospitalizations",
            "Hospitalizations for pneumoconiosis, a lung disease caused by breathing in certain particles or fibers over a prolonged period. Pneumoconiosis usually results from a workplace exposure. The most common causes of pneumoconiosis include asbestos fibers, silica dust, and coal dust. There is no cure, but it can be prevented with appropriate respiratory protection.",
            "Work-related deaths (all industries)",
            "Fatal, work-related injuries. These include deaths resulting from unintentional injuries such as falls, acute poisonings, and motor vehicle crashes that occur during travel for work, and intentional injuries (i.e., homicides and suicides) that occur at work.",
            "Work-related deaths (construction industry), by borough",
            "Construction is the most dangerous industry in NYC, with nearly a third of work-related injury deaths each year.",
            "Work-related deaths (construction industry), Citywide",
            "Construction is the most dangerous industry in NYC, with nearly a third of work-related injury deaths each year. Falls from scaffolds, ladders and roofs are the most common cause of fatal injuries among construction workers.",
